# Multi-hole receive reassembly: when the peer sends out-of-order data
# with multiple gaps, our receiver buffers each disjoint run AND emits
# multi-block SACK options reporting them all (RFC 2018 §4).
#
# This is the receive-side counterpart to the RFC 6675 sender — together,
# both endpoints can recover from multi-hole loss patterns in one RTT
# instead of waiting for retransmit-timeout per hole.
#
# Window math (BUF_CAP=1 MiB, LOCAL_WS_SHIFT=5): out-of-order bytes are
# parked inside the receive ring at their final offsets, i.e. inside the
# window already advertised, so holding them does not shrink the window:
# it stays 1_048_576 >> 5 = 32_768 throughout.

--connect 10.0.0.1:49152 10.0.0.2:80
+0 > SEW 0:0(0) win 65535 <mss 1460, wscale 5, sackOK, TS val * ecr 0>
+.005 < S. 0:0(0) ack 1 win 32768 <mss 1460, wscale 0, sackOK, TS val 1 ecr *>
+0 > . 1:1(0) ack 1 win 32768 <TS val * ecr 1>

# Peer sends 3 disjoint runs out of order:
#   A at [11, 21)    — first hole between rcv_nxt=1 and seq=11
#   B at [31, 41)    — second hole between 21 and 31
#   C at [51, 61)    — third hole between 41 and 51
# Our receiver buffers all three and emits SACK blocks for them
# (most-recently-changed first, per RFC 2018 §4). Emit order is
# TS first then SACK (matches the wire encoder's option ordering).
# TSecr stays 1: out-of-order segments do not update TS.Recent, so the
# ACKs echo the last segment that advanced the left edge (RFC 7323 §4.3).

+.005 < P. 11:21(10) ack 1 win 32768 <TS val 2 ecr *>
# Dup-ACK at rcv_nxt=1 with 1 SACK block describing the held run.
+0 > . 1:1(0) ack 1 win 32768 <TS val * ecr 1, sack 11:21>

+.001 < P. 31:41(10) ack 1 win 32768 <TS val 3 ecr *>
# Dup-ACK with 2 SACK blocks. Most-recent-first ordering: B then A.
+0 > . 1:1(0) ack 1 win 32768 <TS val * ecr 1, sack 31:41 11:21>

+.001 < P. 51:61(10) ack 1 win 32768 <TS val 4 ecr *>
# Dup-ACK with 3 SACK blocks (TS option caps us at 3, not the full 4).
# Most-recent first: C, B, A.
+0 > . 1:1(0) ack 1 win 32768 <TS val * ecr 1, sack 51:61 31:41 11:21>

# The first hole fills: run A becomes in-order and the left edge advances,
# so TS.Recent (and TSecr) moves to this segment's TSval.
+.001 < P. 1:11(10) ack 1 win 32768 <TS val 5 ecr *>
+0 > . 1:1(0) ack 21 win 32768 <TS val * ecr 5, sack 51:61 31:41>
